            <ArticleTitle>Managing the patient with atypical chest pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-73; discussion 173-4 passim</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Since angina and heartburn can feel the same, excluding cardiac disease may be the first order of business. That done, clinical findings and laboratory tests can help identify the esophageal disturbance. Gastric acid reflux, motility disorders, and visceral nerve hypersensitivity--alone or in combination--can cause chest pain, and each may call for a different pharmacologic regimen.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
